@node Overview
@section Overview
@anchor{#overview}
Puppeteer is a Node library which provides a high-level API to control
Chromium or Chrome over the DevTools Protocol.

The Puppeteer API is hierarchical and mirrors the browser structure.

@quotation
@strong{NOTE} On the following diagram, faded entities are not currently
represented in Puppeteer.
@end quotation
@float
@image{https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/746130/40333229-5df5480c-5d0c-11e8-83cb-c3e371de7374,,,puppeteer
overview,png}
@caption{puppeteer overview}
@end float

@itemize
@item
@ref{#class-puppeteer,@code{Puppeteer}} communicates with the browser
using @uref{https://chromedevtools.github.io/devtools-protocol/,DevTools
Protocol}.
@item
@ref{#class-browser,@code{Browser}} instance can own multiple browser
contexts.
@item
@ref{#class-browsercontext,@code{BrowserContext}} instance defines a
browsing session and can own multiple pages.
@item
@ref{#class-page,@code{Page}} has at least one frame: main frame. There
might be other frames created by
@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element/iframe,iframe}
or
@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element/frame,frame}
tags.
@item
@ref{#class-frame,@code{Frame}} has at least one execution context - the
default execution context - where the frame's JavaScript is executed. A
Frame might have additional execution contexts that are associated with
@uref{https://developer.chrome.com/extensions,extensions}.
@item
@ref{#class-worker,@code{Worker}} has a single execution context and
facilitates interacting with
@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_Workers_API,WebWorkers}.
@end itemize

(Diagram source:
@uref{https://docs.google.com/drawings/d/1Q_AM6KYs9kbyLZF-Lpp5mtpAWth73Cq8IKCsWYgi8MM/edit?usp=sharing,link})

@node puppeteer vs puppeteer-core
@section puppeteer vs puppeteer-core
@anchor{#puppeteer-vs-puppeteer-core}
Every release since v1.7.0 we publish two packages: -
@uref{https://www.npmjs.com/package/puppeteer,puppeteer} -
@uref{https://www.npmjs.com/package/puppeteer-core,puppeteer-core}

@code{puppeteer} is a @emph{product} for browser automation. When
installed, it downloads a version of Chromium, which it then drives
using @code{puppeteer-core}. Being an end-user product, @code{puppeteer}
supports a bunch of convenient @code{PUPPETEER_*} env variables to tweak
its behavior.

@code{puppeteer-core} is a @emph{library} to help drive anything that
supports DevTools protocol. @code{puppeteer-core} doesn't download
Chromium when installed. Being a library, @code{puppeteer-core} is fully
driven through its programmatic interface and disregards all the
@code{PUPPETEER_*} env variables.

To sum up, the only differences between @code{puppeteer-core} and
@code{puppeteer} are: - @code{puppeteer-core} doesn't automatically
download Chromium when installed. - @code{puppeteer-core} ignores all
@code{PUPPETEER_*} env variables.

In most cases, you'll be fine using the @code{puppeteer} package.

However, you should use @code{puppeteer-core} if: - you're building
another end-user product or library atop of DevTools protocol. For
example, one might build a PDF generator using @code{puppeteer-core} and
write a custom @code{install.js} script that downloads
@uref{https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/lkgr/headless/README.md,@code{headless_shell}}
instead of Chromium to save disk space. - you're bundling Puppeteer to
use in Chrome Extension / browser with the DevTools protocol where
downloading an additional Chromium binary is unnecessary.

When using @code{puppeteer-core}, remember to change the @emph{include}
line:

@verbatim
const puppeteer = require('puppeteer-core');
@end verbatim

You will then need to call
@ref{#puppeteerconnectoptions,@code{puppeteer.connect([options])}} or
@ref{#puppeteerlaunchoptions,@code{puppeteer.launch([options])}} with an
explicit @code{executablePath} option.

@node Environment Variables
@section Environment Variables
@anchor{#environment-variables}
Puppeteer looks for certain
@uref{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Environment_variable,environment
variables} to aid its operations. If Puppeteer doesn't find them in the
environment during the installation step, a lowercased variant of these
variables will be used from the
@uref{https://docs.npmjs.com/cli/config,npm config}.

@itemize
@item
@code{HTTP_PROXY}, @code{HTTPS_PROXY}, @code{NO_PROXY} - defines HTTP
proxy settings that are used to download and run Chromium.
@item
@code{PUPPETEER_SKIP_CHROMIUM_DOWNLOAD} - do not download bundled
Chromium during installation step.
@item
@code{PUPPETEER_DOWNLOAD_HOST} - overwrite URL prefix that is used to
download Chromium. Note: this includes protocol and might even include
path prefix. Defaults to @code{https://storage.googleapis.com}.
@item
@code{PUPPETEER_CHROMIUM_REVISION} - specify a certain version of
Chromium you'd like Puppeteer to use. See
@ref{#puppeteerlaunchoptions,puppeteer.launch([options])} on how
executable path is inferred. @strong{BEWARE}: Puppeteer is only
@uref{https://github.com/GoogleChrome/puppeteer/#q-why-doesnt-puppeteer-vxxx-work-with-chromium-vyyy,guaranteed
to work} with the bundled Chromium, use at your own risk.
@item
@code{PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH} - specify an executable path to be used
in @code{puppeteer.launch}. See
@ref{#puppeteerlaunchoptions,puppeteer.launch([options])} on how the
executable path is inferred. @strong{BEWARE}: Puppeteer is only
@uref{https://github.com/GoogleChrome/puppeteer/#q-why-doesnt-puppeteer-vxxx-work-with-chromium-vyyy,guaranteed
to work} with the bundled Chromium, use at your own risk.
@end itemize

@quotation
@strong{NOTE} PUPPETEER_* env variables are not accounted for in the
@uref{https://www.npmjs.com/package/puppeteer-core,@code{puppeteer-core}}
package.
@end quotation

@node Working with Chrome Extensions
@section Working with Chrome Extensions
@anchor{#working-with-chrome-extensions}
Puppeteer can be used for testing Chrome Extensions.

@quotation
@strong{NOTE} Extensions in Chrome / Chromium currently only work in
non-headless mode.
@end quotation
The following is code for getting a handle to the
@uref{https://developer.chrome.com/extensions/background_pages,background
page} of an extension whose source is located in @code{./my-extension}:

@verbatim
const puppeteer = require('puppeteer');

(async () => {
  const pathToExtension = require('path').join(__dirname, 'my-extension');
  const browser = await puppeteer.launch({
    headless: false,
    args: [
      `--disable-extensions-except=${pathToExtension}`,
      `--load-extension=${pathToExtension}`
    ]
  });
  const targets = await browser.targets();
  const backgroundPageTarget = targets.find(target => target.type() === 'background_page');
  const backgroundPage = await backgroundPageTarget.page();
  // Test the background page as you would any other page.
  await browser.close();
})();
@end verbatim

@quotation
@strong{NOTE} It is not yet possible to test extension popups or content
scripts.
@end quotation

@node class Puppeteer
@section class: Puppeteer
@anchor{#class-puppeteer}
Puppeteer module provides a method to launch a Chromium instance. The
following is a typical example of using Puppeteer to drive automation:

@verbatim
const puppeteer = require('puppeteer');

puppeteer.launch().then(async browser => {
  const page = await browser.newPage();
  await page.goto('https://www.google.com');
  // other actions...
  await browser.close();
});
@end verbatim

