<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Data_structures#String_type,string}>
Path to a
@uref{https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/master/docs/user_data_dir.md,User
Data Directory}.
@item
@code{env}
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object,Object}>
Specify environment variables that will be visible to the browser.
Defaults to @code{process.env}.
@item
@code{devtools}
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Data_structures#Boolean_type,boolean}>
Whether to auto-open a DevTools panel for each tab. If this option is
@code{true}, the @code{headless} option will be set @code{false}.
@item
@code{pipe}
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Data_structures#Boolean_type,boolean}>
Connects to the browser over a pipe instead of a WebSocket. Defaults to
@code{false}.
@end itemize

@item
returns:
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise,Promise}<@ref{#class-browser,Browser}>>
Promise which resolves to browser instance.
@end itemize

You can use @code{ignoreDefaultArgs} to filter out @code{--mute-audio}
from default arguments:

@verbatim
const browser = await puppeteer.launch({
  ignoreDefaultArgs: ['--mute-audio']
});
@end verbatim

@quotation
@strong{NOTE} Puppeteer can also be used to control the Chrome browser,
but it works best with the version of Chromium it is bundled with. There
is no guarantee it will work with any other version. Use
@code{executablePath} option with extreme caution.

If Google Chrome (rather than Chromium) is preferred, a
@uref{https://www.google.com/chrome/browser/canary.html,Chrome Canary}
or @uref{https://www.chromium.org/getting-involved/dev-channel,Dev
Channel} build is suggested.

In @ref{#puppeteerlaunchoptions,puppeteer.launch([options])} above, any
mention of Chromium also applies to Chrome.

See
@uref{https://www.howtogeek.com/202825/what%E2%80%99s-the-difference-between-chromium-and-chrome/,@code{this article}}
for a description of the differences between Chromium and Chrome.
@uref{https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/lkgr/docs/chromium_browser_vs_google_chrome.md,@code{This article}}
describes some differences for Linux users.
@end quotation

@node class BrowserFetcher
@section class: BrowserFetcher
@anchor{#class-browserfetcher}
BrowserFetcher can download and manage different versions of Chromium.

BrowserFetcher operates on revision strings that specify a precise
version of Chromium, e.g.@ @code{"533271"}. Revision strings can be
obtained from
@uref{http://omahaproxy.appspot.com/,omahaproxy.appspot.com}.

An example of using BrowserFetcher to download a specific version of
Chromium and running Puppeteer against it:

@verbatim
const browserFetcher = puppeteer.createBrowserFetcher();
const revisionInfo = await browserFetcher.download('533271');
const browser = await puppeteer.launch({executablePath: revisionInfo.executablePath})
@end verbatim

@quotation
@strong{NOTE} BrowserFetcher is not designed to work concurrently with
other instances of BrowserFetcher that share the same downloads
directory.
@end quotation

