@node pagesetRequestInterceptionvalue
@subsection page.setRequestInterception(value)
@findex page.setRequestInterception
@anchor{#pagesetrequestinterceptionvalue}
@itemize
@item
@code{value}
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Data_structures#Boolean_type,boolean}>
Whether to enable request interception.
@item
returns:
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise,Promise}>
@end itemize

Activating request interception enables @code{request.abort},
@code{request.continue} and @code{request.respond} methods. This
provides the capability to modify network requests that are made by a
page.

Once request interception is enabled, every request will stall unless
it's continued, responded or aborted. An example of a naïve request
interceptor that aborts all image requests:

@verbatim
const puppeteer = require('puppeteer');

puppeteer.launch().then(async browser => {
  const page = await browser.newPage();
  await page.setRequestInterception(true);
  page.on('request', interceptedRequest => {
    if (interceptedRequest.url().endsWith('.png') || interceptedRequest.url().endsWith('.jpg'))
      interceptedRequest.abort();
    else
      interceptedRequest.continue();
  });
  await page.goto('https://example.com');
  await browser.close();
});
@end verbatim

@quotation
@strong{NOTE} Enabling request interception disables page caching.
@end quotation

