Wait for the @code{selector} to appear in page. If at the moment of
calling the method the @code{selector} already exists, the method will
return immediately. If the selector doesn't appear after the
@code{timeout} milliseconds of waiting, the function will throw.

This method works across navigations:

@verbatim
const puppeteer = require('puppeteer');

puppeteer.launch().then(async browser => {
  const page = await browser.newPage();
  let currentURL;
  page
    .waitForSelector('img')
    .then(() => console.log('First URL with image: ' + currentURL));
  for (currentURL of ['https://example.com', 'https://google.com', 'https://bbc.com'])
    await page.goto(currentURL);
  await browser.close();
});
@end verbatim

