@node class Frame
@section class: Frame
@anchor{#class-frame}
At every point of time, page exposes its current frame tree via the
@ref{#pagemainframe,page.mainFrame()} and
@ref{#framechildframes,frame.childFrames()} methods.

@ref{#class-frame,Frame} object's lifecycle is controlled by three
events, dispatched on the page object: -
@ref{#event-frameattached,`frameattached'} - fired when the frame gets
attached to the page. A Frame can be attached to the page only once. -
@ref{#event-framenavigated,`framenavigated'} - fired when the frame
commits navigation to a different URL. -
@ref{#event-framedetached,`framedetached'} - fired when the frame gets
detached from the page. A Frame can be detached from the page only once.

An example of dumping frame tree:

@verbatim
const puppeteer = require('puppeteer');

puppeteer.launch().then(async browser => {
  const page = await browser.newPage();
  await page.goto('https://www.google.com/chrome/browser/canary.html');
  dumpFrameTree(page.mainFrame(), '');
  await browser.close();

  function dumpFrameTree(frame, indent) {
    console.log(indent + frame.url());
    for (let child of frame.childFrames())
      dumpFrameTree(child, indent + '  ');
  }
});
@end verbatim

An example of getting text from an iframe element:

@verbatim
  const frame = page.frames().find(frame => frame.name() === 'myframe');
  const text = await frame.$eval('.selector', element => element.textContent);
  console.log(text);
@end verbatim

