<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Function,function}|@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Data_structures#String_type,string}>
Function to be evaluated in the page context
@item
@code{...args}
<@dots{}@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/JSON/stringify#Description,Serializable}|@ref{#class-jshandle,JSHandle}>
Arguments to pass to @code{pageFunction}
@item
returns:
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise,Promise}<@ref{#class-jshandle,JSHandle}>>
Promise which resolves to the return value of @code{pageFunction} as
in-page object (JSHandle)
@end itemize

The only difference between @code{frame.evaluate} and
@code{frame.evaluateHandle} is that @code{frame.evaluateHandle} returns
in-page object (JSHandle).

If the function, passed to the @code{frame.evaluateHandle}, returns a
@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise,Promise},
then @code{frame.evaluateHandle} would wait for the promise to resolve
and return its value.

@verbatim
const aWindowHandle = await frame.evaluateHandle(() => Promise.resolve(window));
aWindowHandle; // Handle for the window object.
@end verbatim

A string can also be passed in instead of a function.

@verbatim
const aHandle = await frame.evaluateHandle('document'); // Handle for the 'document'.
@end verbatim

@ref{#class-jshandle,JSHandle} instances can be passed as arguments to
the @code{frame.evaluateHandle}:

@verbatim
const aHandle = await frame.evaluateHandle(() => document.body);
const resultHandle = await frame.evaluateHandle(body => body.innerHTML, aHandle);
console.log(await resultHandle.jsonValue());
await resultHandle.dispose();
@end verbatim

