@node class ExecutionContext
@section class: ExecutionContext
@anchor{#class-executioncontext}
The class represents a context for JavaScript execution. A
@ref{#class-page,Page} might have many execution contexts: - each
@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element/iframe,frame}
has ``default'' execution context that is always created after frame is
attached to DOM. This context is returned by the
@ref{#frameexecutioncontext,@code{frame.executionContext()}} method. -
@uref{https://developer.chrome.com/extensions,Extensions}'s content
scripts create additional execution contexts.

Besides pages, execution contexts can be found in
@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_Workers_API,workers}.

@menu
* executionContextevaluatepageFunction[@dots{}args]::
* executionContextevaluateHandlepageFunction[@dots{}args]::
* executionContextframe::
* executionContextqueryObjectsprototypeHandle::
@end menu

@node executionContextevaluatepageFunction[@dots{}args]
@subsection executionContext.evaluate(pageFunction[,@dots{}args])
@findex executionContext.evaluate
@anchor{#executioncontextevaluatepagefunction-args}
@itemize
@item
@code{pageFunction}
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Function,function}|@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Data_structures#String_type,string}>
Function to be evaluated in @code{executionContext}
@item
@code{...args}
<@dots{}@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/JSON/stringify#Description,Serializable}|@ref{#class-jshandle,JSHandle}>
Arguments to pass to @code{pageFunction}
@item
returns:
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise,Promise}<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/JSON/stringify#Description,Serializable}>>
Promise which resolves to the return value of @code{pageFunction}
@end itemize

If the function passed to the @code{executionContext.evaluate} returns a
@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise,Promise},
then @code{executionContext.evaluate} would wait for the promise to
resolve and return its value.

If the function passed to the @code{executionContext.evaluate} returns a
non-@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/JSON/stringify#Description,Serializable}
value, then @code{executionContext.evaluate} resolves to
@code{undefined}. DevTools Protocol also supports transferring some
additional values that are not serializable by @code{JSON}: @code{-0},
@code{NaN}, @code{Infinity}, @code{-Infinity}, and bigint literals.

@verbatim
const executionContext = await page.mainFrame().executionContext();
const result = await executionContext.evaluate(() => Promise.resolve(8 * 7));
console.log(result); // prints "56"
@end verbatim

A string can also be passed in instead of a function.

@verbatim
console.log(await executionContext.evaluate('1 + 2')); // prints "3"
@end verbatim

@ref{#class-jshandle,JSHandle} instances can be passed as arguments to
the @code{executionContext.evaluate}:

@verbatim
const oneHandle = await executionContext.evaluateHandle(() => 1);
const twoHandle = await executionContext.evaluateHandle(() => 2);
const result = await executionContext.evaluate((a, b) => a + b, oneHandle, twoHandle);
await oneHandle.dispose();
await twoHandle.dispose();
console.log(result); // prints '3'.
@end verbatim

@node executionContextevaluateHandlepageFunction[@dots{}args]
@subsection executionContext.evaluateHandle(pageFunction[,@dots{}args])
@findex executionContext.evaluateHandle
@anchor{#executioncontextevaluatehandlepagefunction-args}
@itemize
@item
@code{pageFunction}
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Function,function}|@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Data_structures#String_type,string}>
Function to be evaluated in the @code{executionContext}
@item
@code{...args}
<@dots{}@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/JSON/stringify#Description,Serializable}|@ref{#class-jshandle,JSHandle}>
Arguments to pass to @code{pageFunction}
@item
returns:
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise,Promise}<@ref{#class-jshandle,JSHandle}>>
Promise which resolves to the return value of @code{pageFunction} as
in-page object (JSHandle)
@end itemize

The only difference between @code{executionContext.evaluate} and
@code{executionContext.evaluateHandle} is that
@code{executionContext.evaluateHandle} returns in-page object
(JSHandle).

If the function passed to the @code{executionContext.evaluateHandle}
returns a
@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise,Promise},
then @code{executionContext.evaluateHandle} would wait for the promise
to resolve and return its value.

@verbatim
const context = await page.mainFrame().executionContext();
const aHandle = await context.evaluateHandle(() => Promise.resolve(self));
aHandle; // Handle for the global object.
@end verbatim

A string can also be passed in instead of a function.

@verbatim
const aHandle = await context.evaluateHandle('1 + 2'); // Handle for the '3' object.
@end verbatim

@ref{#class-jshandle,JSHandle} instances can be passed as arguments to
the @code{executionContext.evaluateHandle}:

@verbatim
const aHandle = await context.evaluateHandle(() => document.body);
const resultHandle = await context.evaluateHandle(body => body.innerHTML, aHandle);
console.log(await resultHandle.jsonValue()); // prints body's innerHTML
await aHandle.dispose();
await resultHandle.dispose();
@end verbatim

