@node executionContextqueryObjectsprototypeHandle
@subsection executionContext.queryObjects(prototypeHandle)
@findex executionContext.queryObjects
@anchor{#executioncontextqueryobjectsprototypehandle}
@itemize
@item
@code{prototypeHandle} <@ref{#class-jshandle,JSHandle}> A handle to the
object prototype.
@item
returns:
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise,Promise}<@ref{#class-jshandle,JSHandle}>>
A handle to an array of objects with this prototype
@end itemize

The method iterates the JavaScript heap and finds all the objects with
the given prototype.

@verbatim
// Create a Map object
await page.evaluate(() => window.map = new Map());
// Get a handle to the Map object prototype
const mapPrototype = await page.evaluateHandle(() => Map.prototype);
// Query all map instances into an array
const mapInstances = await page.queryObjects(mapPrototype);
// Count amount of map objects in heap
const count = await page.evaluate(maps => maps.length, mapInstances);
await mapInstances.dispose();
await mapPrototype.dispose();
@end verbatim

@node class JSHandle
@section class: JSHandle
@anchor{#class-jshandle}
JSHandle represents an in-page JavaScript object. JSHandles can be
created with the
@ref{#pageevaluatehandlepagefunction-args,page.evaluateHandle} method.

@verbatim
const windowHandle = await page.evaluateHandle(() => window);
// ...
@end verbatim

JSHandle prevents the referenced JavaScript object being garbage
collected unless the handle is @ref{#jshandledispose,disposed}.
JSHandles are auto-disposed when their origin frame gets navigated or
the parent context gets destroyed.

JSHandle instances can be used as arguments in
@ref{#pageevalselector-pagefunction-args,@code{page.$eval()}},
@ref{#pageevaluatepagefunction-args,@code{page.evaluate()}} and
@ref{#pageevaluatehandlepagefunction-args,@code{page.evaluateHandle}}
methods.

