@node requestabort[errorCode]
@subsection request.abort([errorCode])
@findex request.abort
@anchor{#requestaborterrorcode}
@itemize
@item
@code{errorCode}
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Data_structures#String_type,string}>
Optional error code. Defaults to @code{failed}, could be one of the
following:
@itemize
@item
@code{aborted} - An operation was aborted (due to user action)
@item
@code{accessdenied} - Permission to access a resource, other than the
network, was denied
@item
@code{addressunreachable} - The IP address is unreachable. This usually
means that there is no route to the specified host or network.
@item
@code{blockedbyclient} - The client chose to block the request.
@item
@code{blockedbyresponse} - The request failed because the response was
delivered along with requirements which are not met (`X-Frame-Options'
and `Content-Security-Policy' ancestor checks, for instance).
@item
@code{connectionaborted} - A connection timed out as a result of not
receiving an ACK for data sent.
@item
@code{connectionclosed} - A connection was closed (corresponding to a
TCP FIN).
@item
@code{connectionfailed} - A connection attempt failed.
@item
@code{connectionrefused} - A connection attempt was refused.
@item
@code{connectionreset} - A connection was reset (corresponding to a TCP
RST).
@item
@code{internetdisconnected} - The Internet connection has been lost.
@item
@code{namenotresolved} - The host name could not be resolved.
@item
@code{timedout} - An operation timed out.
@item
@code{failed} - A generic failure occurred.
@end itemize

@item
returns:
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise,Promise}>
@end itemize

Aborts request. To use this, request interception should be enabled with
@code{page.setRequestInterception}. Exception is immediately thrown if
the request interception is not enabled.

@node requestcontinue[overrides]
@subsection request.continue([overrides])
@findex request.continue
@anchor{#requestcontinueoverrides}
@itemize
@item
@code{overrides}
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object,Object}>
Optional request overwrites, which can be one of the following:
@itemize
@item
@code{url}
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Data_structures#String_type,string}>
If set, the request url will be changed. This is not a redirect. The
request will be silently forwarded to the new url. For example, the
address bar will show the original url.
@item
@code{method}
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Data_structures#String_type,string}>
If set changes the request method (e.g.@ @code{GET} or @code{POST})
@item
@code{postData}
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Data_structures#String_type,string}>
If set changes the post data of request
@item
@code{headers}
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Object,Object}>
If set changes the request HTTP headers
@end itemize

@item
returns:
<@uref{https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise,Promise}>
@end itemize

Continues request with optional request overrides. To use this, request
interception should be enabled with @code{page.setRequestInterception}.
Exception is immediately thrown if the request interception is not
enabled.

@verbatim
await page.setRequestInterception(true);
page.on('request', request => {
  // Override headers
  const headers = Object.assign({}, request.headers(), {
    foo: 'bar', // set "foo" header
    origin: undefined, // remove "origin" header
  });
  request.continue({headers});
});
@end verbatim

